// questionBank3.js — Days 6-10
const QB3 = {};

QB3.day6 = {
junior: [
{q:'What is Backward Scheduling?',a:'Starts from requested delivery date, calculates backwards through transit time, loading time, pick/pack time to find the material availability date. If result is in the past, system switches to forward scheduling.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Forward Scheduling?',a:'Starts from today (or material availability date), calculates forward through pick/pack, loading, transit to find the earliest possible delivery date. Used when backward scheduling produces a past date.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What are the four lead times used in delivery scheduling?',a:'Transit time (route-based, SP to customer), Loading time (shipping point config), Pick/Pack time (shipping point config), Transport planning time (shipping point config). All must be correctly maintained.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Where is transit time maintained?',a:'On the Route. Route is determined from Shipping Point + Ship-to customer (or zone). Transit time = days goods spend in transport. Maintained in route config.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the Material Availability Date (MAD)?',a:'The date by which the material must be available in the warehouse to meet the delivery schedule. Calculated by backward scheduling from the requested delivery date minus all lead times.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What happens when backward scheduling gives a past date?',a:'System automatically switches to forward scheduling, starting from today. The confirmed delivery date will be later than the customer requested. This is visible on the schedule line.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Backorder?',a:'When ATP confirms less than the ordered quantity. E.g., ordered 100, available 80. Delivery created for 80, remaining 20 stays on the order as an open schedule line for future delivery.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How is Shipping Point determined?',a:'Three inputs: Shipping Condition (customer master or doc type) + Loading Group (material master) + Delivering Plant. Configuration in OVL2 maps the combination to a shipping point.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Route and how is it determined?',a:'Transport path from shipping point to customer. Determined by: Country/Zone of shipping point + Country/Zone of ship-to. Controls transit time, transportation planning, and freight calculation.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can you reschedule a delivery after PGI?',a:'Not directly. Must: (1) Reverse PGI, (2) Change delivery dates, (3) Re-post PGI. PGI locks the delivery for financial integrity.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the Delivery Block?',a:'A block that prevents delivery creation from a sales order. Can be set at header or item level. Reasons: credit hold, export compliance, management approval. Must be removed before delivery processing.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is partial delivery tolerance?',a:'Customer master setting controlling whether partial deliveries are acceptable. Options: unlimited partial deliveries, one partial then complete, no partial deliveries allowed.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the complete delivery indicator?',a:'When set on customer master: ALL items must be fully available before ANY delivery is created. System waits until everything is ready. Prevents sending multiple small shipments.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Delivery Group?',a:'Groups order items that should be delivered together. Items with same delivery group number combine into one delivery. Different groups create separate deliveries.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the VL06O transaction?',a:'Delivery Monitor. Lists all open deliveries for mass processing: picking, packing, PGI, printing. Used by warehouse operations for daily delivery management.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is picking in the delivery process?',a:'Physical retrieval of goods from warehouse storage. Picking creates a transfer order (WM) or transfer requirement. Confirmed on delivery as picked quantity.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is packing in the delivery process?',a:'After picking: grouping items into handling units (boxes, pallets). Optional step. Packing data appears on delivery note and shipping documents.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the delivery due list (VL04)?',a:'List of sales orders ready for delivery creation. Shows orders where MAD has been reached and stock is available. Used to process multiple deliveries in batch.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Shipment document?',a:'Optional logistics document that groups multiple deliveries into one transport (truck, container). Links deliveries to carrier, tracks transport status. Transaction VT01N.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How do you determine if scheduling is causing wrong delivery dates?',a:'Check each lead time individually: transit time (route), loading time (SP), pick/pack time (SP). Verify the route determination. Check if backward or forward scheduling was used. Schedule line detail shows the calculation breakdown.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the Transportation Planning Point?',a:'Organizational unit responsible for arranging transport. Assigned to shipping points. Relevant when shipment documents are used to consolidate deliveries onto transport vehicles.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the Goods Issue Date vs Delivery Date?',a:'Delivery date = when goods arrive at customer. GI date = when goods leave your warehouse. GI date = delivery date minus transit time. Two different dates, often confused.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Over-delivery tolerance?',a:'Material master or customer master setting. Allows delivering MORE than ordered (e.g., 5% over). Used for bulk goods where exact quantity is impractical. System allows PGI for slightly higher quantity.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Under-delivery tolerance?',a:'Allows delivering LESS than ordered and closing the order (no backorder). E.g., ordered 100, deliver 95, order is considered complete if within tolerance. Prevents small remaining balances.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Returnable Transport Packaging (RTP)?',a:'Pallets, containers, or crates that are delivered with goods but returned by customer. Tracked separately. Not invoiced (or invoiced as deposit). Managed as special stock.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What happens to inventory at Goods Issue?',a:'Three simultaneous effects: (1) Unrestricted stock reduced in the storage location, (2) COGS posted in FI (debit COGS, credit inventory), (3) Delivery status changes to "goods issued."',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can you create a delivery without a sales order?',a:'Not in standard SD flow — delivery references a sales order. Exception: STO deliveries reference a PO, and some custom processes may allow standalone deliveries, but not recommended.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the delivery split criteria?',a:'Rules determining when one order creates multiple deliveries. Split by: different ship-to partners, different plants, different shipping points, delivery group, or partial availability.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is batch split at delivery?',a:'When one order line is fulfilled from multiple batches. E.g., 100 units: 60 from batch A, 40 from batch B. Delivery line splits into sub-items, each with its batch. Total matches order quantity.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the proof of delivery (POD) process?',a:'Customer confirms receipt (electronically or physically). POD updates delivery status. Can trigger billing (billing only after POD confirmation). Used for high-value or compliance-critical shipments.',type:'conceptual'},
],
senior: [
{q:'Client reports delivery dates are consistently 3 days late vs what was promised at order entry. Root cause?',a:'Systematic scheduling error. Check: (1) Transit time on route — is it accurate? (2) Loading time on shipping point — realistic for their operation? (3) Pick/pack time — does warehouse actually meet this? (4) Is the system using backward or forward scheduling? (5) Calendar: are factory calendar non-working days causing shifts? Most common: transit time not updated after carrier change.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Client wants to guarantee same-day delivery for premium customers but standard 5-day for regular. Configuration?',a:'Different Shipping Conditions per customer group. Premium customers: shipping condition with zero transit/loading time + dedicated shipping point. Route for premium has express transit time. Regular customers: standard shipping condition with normal lead times. Two different scheduling paths from same plant.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'High-volume client processes 2000 deliveries/day. VL06O is slow. Optimization?',a:'(1) Split processing by shipping point or plant (parallel runs). (2) Schedule background jobs for mass PGI (RSNAST00 for outputs, VL06P for PGI). (3) Reduce delivery split — merge where possible. (4) Check database indexes on LIKP/LIPS tables. (5) Consider WM integration optimization if warehouse management is active.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Design a process where goods must pass quality inspection after picking but before PGI. Standard SAP approach?',a:'Pick goods → Transfer to QI storage location → QM inspection lot created → Inspection decision (pass/fail) → If pass: transfer to GI staging area → PGI. Requires WM/QM integration. QI storage type in WM config. Inspection lot type for delivery-triggered inspection.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Customer orders 500 units. Plant A has 300, Plant B has 200. How does delivery work?',a:'If both plants assigned to the SalesOrg+DC: system can create two deliveries (one per plant) or one delivery if cross-plant delivery is configured. Typically: two separate deliveries with different shipping points. Customer receives two shipments. Combined on one invoice via collective billing.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'After go-live, warehouse team says pick/pack times in SAP dont match reality. Impact and fix?',a:'Impact: delivery promises to customers are wrong (too optimistic or too conservative). Fix: (1) Measure actual pick/pack times by product type and volume. (2) Update shipping point config with realistic values. (3) Consider different shipping points for different product types if times vary significantly. (4) Re-run scheduling for open orders.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Client needs proof-of-delivery before billing. Customer must confirm receipt electronically. End-to-end process?',a:'(1) Delivery created and shipped. (2) Customer receives POD form (EDI, portal, or mobile app). (3) Customer confirms receipt (quantity, condition). (4) POD confirmation posted on delivery (VL06P or electronic). (5) Only confirmed deliveries appear on billing due list (VF04). (6) Copy control requirement checks POD status before billing allowed.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Multi-plant delivery consolidation: customer wants one delivery from three plants. Possible?',a:'Not standard — delivery is per shipping point/plant. Alternatives: (1) STO to consolidate stock at one plant first, then one delivery. (2) Cross-docking at a distribution center. (3) Shipment document groups multiple deliveries onto one transport (customer receives one truck, multiple deliveries). Each has different cost and time implications.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Delivery split is creating too many small deliveries, increasing freight costs. How to reduce?',a:'(1) Increase partial delivery tolerance — consolidate smaller quantities. (2) Use delivery groups to force items together. (3) Complete delivery indicator — wait for full availability. (4) Delivery date consolidation — batch deliveries to same dates via billing date calendar. (5) Minimum delivery quantity setting on customer master.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Goods Issue reversal: 500 units PGI posted, customer asks to change to 450. Walk through the reversal.',a:'(1) VL09: reverse PGI → inventory restored, COGS reversed. (2) VA02: change delivery qty from 500 to 450 (or VL02N on delivery directly). (3) VL02N: repost PGI for 450. (4) Remaining 50: stays on order as open if not rejected, or reject to close. (5) If invoice already created for 500: must cancel invoice (VF11) and rebill for 450.',type:'scenario'},
],
};

QB3.day7 = {
junior: [
{q:'What does ATP stand for and what does it calculate?',a:'Available-to-Promise. Calculates: Current Stock + Planned Receipts (POs, production orders) − Committed Requirements (other SOs, reservations) = Available quantity on the requested date.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Checking Group?',a:'Material master field (Sales Org 2 view) that controls which ATP checking rule/procedure is used. Different checking groups can include/exclude different stock types and receipt categories.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the Checking Rule?',a:'Defines which transaction types trigger ATP (sales orders, deliveries, STOs). Combined with checking group to determine the full ATP scope.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What stock elements can be included in ATP?',a:'Unrestricted stock, quality inspection stock (optional), blocked stock (optional), purchase orders, production orders, planned orders (optional), safety stock (optional). Configured via scope of check.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Replenishment Lead Time (RLT)?',a:'Total time to replenish material: production time + GR processing + goods receipt days. Orders requesting dates beyond RLT are auto-confirmed because replenishment is assumed possible.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the scope of check (OVZ9)?',a:'Determines which supply and demand elements are considered in ATP. E.g., include POs and production orders as supply, include other SOs and reservations as demand. Configured per checking group.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What happens when ATP fails?',a:'Requested quantity not available on requested date. System confirms a partial quantity or a later date (depending on config). Schedule line shows confirmed qty and date vs requested.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the one-time delivery vs ATP check?',a:'ATP can run at order entry (sales order) and/or at delivery creation. Double-checking ensures stock hasnt been consumed between order and delivery.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Credit Management?',a:'Controls how much financial exposure a customer can have. Credit limit minus (open items + open orders + open deliveries) = available credit. Exceeding limit triggers blocks.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Credit Check Point?',a:'Configurable moments when credit is checked: at order entry, at delivery creation, at goods issue. Different check types at different points (static, dynamic).',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Static Credit Check?',a:'Simple: total exposure vs credit limit. If exposure > limit, block. No time-based analysis. Quick but less nuanced.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Dynamic Credit Check?',a:'Time-horizon based: calculates exposure over a configurable period (e.g., next 90 days). More accurate for businesses with seasonal ordering patterns. More complex to configure.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Where is the Credit Limit maintained?',a:'Credit Management area of the customer master (FD32/FD33). Set per credit control area. Can be managed centrally or distributed.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What happens when a credit check fails?',a:'Depends on configuration: Warning (user can override), Delivery block (automatic), Order block (cannot save). Most common: delivery block + notification to credit analyst.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Who releases a credit block?',a:'Credit analyst or manager with appropriate authorization. Release via VKM1/VKM3 (credit management worklist). Can also be released directly on the order or delivery.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is FSCM Credit Management in S/4HANA?',a:'Enhanced credit management: weighted exposure categories, dynamic scoring based on payment behavior, external credit bureau integration, real-time exposure calculation. Replaces classic SD credit management.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the difference between credit exposure and open items?',a:'Open items = invoices not yet paid (FI receivables). Exposure = open items + open orders + open deliveries (potential future receivables). Exposure is always >= open items.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Safety Stock and how does it relate to ATP?',a:'Minimum stock level maintained for emergencies. If safety stock is excluded from ATP scope: that stock is protected from regular customer orders. If included: it can be consumed, defeating its purpose.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Product Allocation?',a:'When demand exceeds supply, product allocation controls WHO gets stock, not just first-come-first-served. Allocation by customer, region, priority. Used for new product launches or supply shortages.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the ATP confirmation process?',a:'System checks availability → confirms full, partial, or zero quantity → schedule line updated with confirmed qty and confirmed date. User sees requested vs confirmed on the order.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the difference between individual and collective ATP?',a:'Individual: checked per order item. Collective: can consider multiple items together for combined availability. Relevant for MTO where stock is earmarked for specific orders.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is backorder processing (V_RA)?',a:'Report that re-checks ATP for existing orders with unconfirmed quantities. When new stock arrives: reallocates to waiting orders based on priority or FIFO. Run periodically or after stock receipt.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the credit control area?',a:'Organizational unit for credit management. Assigned to one or more Company Codes. Contains credit limit settings, risk categories, and credit analyst assignments.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is automatic credit control vs manual?',a:'Automatic: system applies configured rules without user intervention (blocks when limit exceeded). Manual: credit analyst reviews and decides case by case (no automatic blocks).',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the difference between ATP at order vs delivery?',a:'Order ATP: reserves/confirms stock when order is created. Delivery ATP: re-checks at delivery creation (stock may have changed). Both can be active. Delivery check catches consumed stock.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Fixed Date and Qty in ATP?',a:'When checked: requested delivery date and quantity are fixed. System confirms only on that date — no partial confirmation, no alternative dates. Either full qty on that date or zero.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can credit limit be zero?',a:'Yes — effectively blocks all orders for that customer. Used for prepayment-only customers or customers under credit hold. Orders blocked until credit analyst intervenes.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the payment history in credit decisions?',a:'Customer payment behavior (average days late, oldest open item, payment frequency) influences dynamic credit checks. Good payers may get automatic credit increases; bad payers get flagged.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the credit exposure formula?',a:'Credit Exposure = Open AR Items + Open Order Values + Open Delivery Values + Special Liabilities. Available Credit = Credit Limit − Credit Exposure.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the credit master sheet (FD32)?',a:'Displays a customers complete credit picture: credit limit, total exposure broken down by open items/orders/deliveries, payment history, risk category, credit analyst assignment.',type:'conceptual'},
],
senior: [
{q:'ATP keeps over-promising delivery dates. Customer complains about late deliveries. Systematic investigation?',a:'(1) Check RLT — if too short, system auto-confirms unrealistically. (2) Check scope of check — are unfirmed planned orders included as supply? They may not materialize. (3) Check safety stock inclusion — are orders consuming buffer stock? (4) Check if backorder processing is running — may be re-confirming without real stock. (5) Compare ATP confirmed qty vs actual delivered qty over last 30 days to quantify the gap.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Credit check passes at order but blocks at delivery for the same customer and amount. Why?',a:'Between order creation and delivery creation: (1) Other orders from same customer increased exposure, (2) Payments not yet posted (receivables still open), (3) Exchange rate change affected exposure calculation for foreign currency customers, (4) Credit limit was reduced. The check is dynamic — exposure changes in real-time.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Client migrating from classic credit management to FSCM. Key differences and migration risks?',a:'Differences: FSCM uses business partner (not customer), weighted exposure categories (not flat sum), dynamic scoring, bureau integration. Risks: (1) Data mapping — customer credit data to BP format, (2) Different calculation logic may change who gets blocked, (3) Custom credit reports need rewriting, (4) User training on new transactions. Parallel run recommended: both systems active during transition.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Design ATP for a company that sells from 3 plants, wants multi-plant availability check, and allows substitution between plants.',a:'aATP (advanced ATP) in S/4HANA or multi-plant ATP in ECC. Config: (1) Checking group includes all 3 plants in scope, (2) Substitution rules: if Plant A has zero, check Plant B, then Plant C, (3) Priority by proximity to customer (shipping cost optimization), (4) Transportation lane times differ per plant — affects delivery date. Trade-off: most available vs closest vs cheapest.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Customer has ₹50L credit limit. Current exposure ₹48L. New order for ₹5L. Business says "approve it, theyre a good customer." Your recommendation?',a:'(1) Check payment history — are they actually current? (2) Check oldest open item — any disputes? (3) The order puts them ₹3L over limit. Options: (a) Temporary limit increase with documented approval, (b) Partial release — deliver ₹2L now, rest after payment received, (c) Prepayment for the overage. (4) Document the decision for audit trail. Never permanently increase limit without formal credit review.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Product allocation scenario: new phone launch, 10,000 units available, 50,000 units ordered across 200 customers. Design the allocation.',a:'(1) Define allocation categories: VIP customers (40%), key accounts (30%), regular (30%). (2) Within each category: FIFO or proportional by order quantity. (3) Allocation period: weekly refresh as production delivers more units. (4) Override mechanism for strategic accounts (with approval). (5) Communication: proactively inform customers of allocated qty and expected delivery for remainder.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'ATP shows stock available but warehouse says shelf is empty. Reconciliation approach?',a:'(1) Check MB52: what does SAP say is in stock? If SAP shows stock: physical count is wrong OR goods were moved without SAP transaction. (2) Check recent movement types: was there an unposted GI? (3) Check if stock is blocked or in QI (not unrestricted). (4) Check WM vs MM stock reconciliation if WM is active. (5) Cycle count → adjust stock → investigate root cause (missing postings, wrong storage bins).',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Client wants credit management to automatically increase limits by 10% for customers with perfect payment history. Possible?',a:'FSCM supports rule-based limit adjustments. Configure: scoring model where on-time payment increases score, score threshold triggers automatic limit increase (with cap). Classic credit: not standard — need custom program to evaluate payment history and adjust FD32 limits periodically. Either way: set maximum auto-increase cap and audit trail.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'aATP in S/4HANA vs classic ATP — when does each apply and what are the trade-offs?',a:'Classic ATP: simpler, adequate for single-plant, basic availability. aATP: multi-plant sourcing, rule-based substitution, backorder prioritization by customer importance, product allocation integration. Trade-offs: aATP needs more config, licensing cost, and BW integration for allocation. Use classic for simple businesses, aATP for complex supply chains.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Credit management across 5 company codes with one group limit. Customer buys from all 5. How to prevent exceeding group limit while allowing individual CC limits?',a:'Credit control area spanning all 5 CCs. Individual limits per CC (e.g., ₹10L each). Group limit at credit control area level (e.g., ₹30L total, not 5×10=50L). System checks: individual CC limit AND overall group limit. Either breach = block. Requires central credit management with visibility across all CCs.',type:'scenario'},
],
};

QB3.day8 = {junior:[
{q:'What is Output Determination?',a:'Controls what documents get printed, emailed, faxed, or sent via EDI, and when. Configured via NACE. Uses the same condition technique as pricing — access sequences find condition records.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What are the main output types in SD?',a:'BA00 (order confirmation), LD00 (delivery note), RD00 (invoice), GS00 (goods issue slip). Each has its own access sequence, processing routine, medium, and dispatch time.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is dispatch time 1?',a:'Immediately processed when the document is saved. The output (print, email, EDI) is triggered automatically at save. Most common for order confirmations.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is dispatch time 2?',a:'Batch processing. Output is generated but not sent until the background job RSNAST00 runs. Requires scheduled job. Most common cause of "stuck" outputs.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is dispatch time 4?',a:'Do not send. Output is generated but held for manual processing. User must explicitly trigger sending. Used for outputs that need review before release.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is NACE?',a:'Transaction to configure output determination. Maintains output types per application area: V1 (sales), V2 (shipping/delivery), V3 (billing). Each area has its own output types and procedures.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the output medium?',a:'How the output is delivered: 1 = Print, 2 = Fax, 5 = External send (email), 6 = EDI. Each output type can have different mediums for different customers.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a processing routine in output?',a:'The form/program that generates the output document. Can be: SAPscript form, SmartForms form, or Adobe PDF form. Assigned to the output type in NACE.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is VV31/VV32/VV33?',a:'Output condition record maintenance. VV31 = create, VV32 = change, VV33 = display. Similar to VK11/12/13 for pricing but specifically for output condition records.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What does "no recipient found" mean in output processing?',a:'The output type was triggered but the partner function has no communication details (email address, fax number, printer). Fix: maintain communication details in customer master.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the output processing log?',a:'Shows the status of each output: processed, error, not processed. Includes error messages explaining why output failed. Accessible from the document → output tab → processing log.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can multiple outputs be generated for one document?',a:'Yes. A sales order can trigger: customer confirmation (email) + internal copy (print) + EDI to warehouse + management report. Multiple output types can be active simultaneously.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is RSNAST00?',a:'Background program that processes batch outputs (dispatch time 2). Must be scheduled as a regular job. If not scheduled, all batch outputs accumulate unprocessed. Most common output issue.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is SmartForms vs SAPscript?',a:'SAPscript = legacy form technology (text-based). SmartForms = newer, graphical form builder (WYSIWYG). Adobe Forms = newest (PDF-based). S/4HANA promotes Adobe Forms.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is SCOT?',a:'SAPconnect configuration. Manages email sending from SAP. Must be configured with SMTP server details for email outputs to work. Missing SCOT config = emails dont send.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How does output determination relate to partner functions?',a:'Output is sent TO a specific partner function. E.g., order confirmation sent to Sold-to, delivery note to Ship-to. The partner communication details (email, fax) determine the delivery method.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is an EDI output?',a:'Electronic Data Interchange. Output sent as an IDoc (intermediate document) to the customers system. Uses partner profiles (WE20), ports, and RFC connections. Common for large B2B customers.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a requirement routine in output?',a:'ABAP logic that determines whether an output should be created. E.g., only send confirmation if order value > ₹1000. If requirement not met, output type is suppressed.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the condition technique in output?',a:'Same concept as pricing: access sequences search condition tables to find matching condition records. Instead of finding a price, they find an output type with its medium and dispatch time.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can output be manually triggered?',a:'Yes. Users can manually add output types to a document and trigger processing. Useful for re-sending failed outputs or sending ad-hoc communications.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the difference between output at order vs delivery vs billing?',a:'Three separate output determination procedures: V1 (sales/orders), V2 (shipping/deliveries), V3 (billing). Each has its own output types, access sequences, and forms. Independent configuration.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is an output determination procedure?',a:'Assigned per document type. Lists which output types are checked for that document. Like a pricing procedure but for outputs. Maintained in NACE.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the print preview?',a:'Allows viewing the output document before actually printing/sending. Available from the document → output → print preview. Useful for checking form layout and data accuracy.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is BRF+ in context of output?',a:'Business Rule Framework Plus. In S/4HANA, replaces condition technique for output determination. Allows more complex rules (if/then/else logic) beyond what condition technique supports.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How do you suppress output for specific document types?',a:'Options: (1) Dont maintain condition records for that doc type, (2) Use requirement routine that checks doc type and returns false, (3) Dont assign the output type in the output determination procedure for that doc type.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the output status?',a:'Tracks processing: 0 = not processed, 1 = successfully processed, 2 = incorrectly processed (error). Status visible on document output tab. Errors can be investigated via processing log.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is repeat processing of output?',a:'Re-sending an already processed output. Useful when original was lost or needs to be re-sent. Mark the output for repeat processing and trigger reprocessing.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the access sequence for output?',a:'Like pricing: hierarchical search. Might check: Doc Type + Customer → Doc Type only → Customer only → Blank. First match determines which output type with which settings.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is message control vs output management?',a:'Message control = ECC term for output determination (NACE/condition technique). Output management = S/4HANA term (BRF+/PPF). Different technology, same business purpose.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Where do you check if an IDoc was created from EDI output?',a:'WE05 (IDoc list). Filter by message type, date, partner. Shows IDoc status: green (sent), yellow (created), red (error). Drill into IDoc for content and error details.',type:'conceptual'},
],
senior:[
{q:'Post-go-live: thousands of output messages stuck at status 0 (not processed). Systematic resolution?',a:'(1) Check dispatch time: if batch (2), is RSNAST00 scheduled? If not, schedule it. (2) For email outputs: is SCOT configured with SMTP? (3) For EDI: check partner profiles WE20, IDoc status WE05. (4) For print: check printer assignments and spool. (5) Run RSNAST00 in test mode with error log to identify failure categories. (6) Fix root causes by category, then mass-process.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Client needs different invoice formats for domestic, export, and intercompany customers. Architecture?',a:'Three output types for billing: ZRD1 (domestic), ZRD2 (export), ZRD3 (IC). Each linked to a different form (different layouts, languages, legal requirements). Access sequence uses customer country or account group to determine which output type. Single output determination procedure handles all three.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Migration from SAPscript to Adobe Forms. 30 forms affected. Approach?',a:'(1) Inventory all 30 SAPscript forms with their output types. (2) Create equivalent Adobe Forms (preserve layout/data). (3) Update NACE: change processing routine from SAPscript program/form to Adobe driver/form. (4) Condition records and access sequences stay unchanged. (5) Test each form with representative data. (6) Phased rollout: go form-by-form, not big bang.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client wants email confirmation with PDF attachment to customer + separate EDI message to warehouse + print copy for filing — all from one order save. Config?',a:'Three output types on one order: (1) BA01 — medium 5 (email), Adobe form for PDF attachment, dispatch time 1 (immediate). (2) BA02 — medium 6 (EDI), IDoc type ORDERS, dispatch time 1. (3) BA03 — medium 1 (print), SAPscript/SmartForms, dispatch time 1 or 2. Three condition records for the same document. All three process independently.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Output determination works but emails arrive without the PDF attachment. Just plain email. Diagnosis?',a:'(1) Check output type medium settings — is PDF attachment configured (not just plain text)? (2) Check form output type — Adobe Forms generates PDF, SAPscript generates plain text by default. (3) Check SCOT: email format settings (HTML vs RAW). (4) Check partner communication details: email address format. (5) Check spool: is the PDF being generated in spool before sending?',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Client has 50 customers on EDI. 5 different EDI formats needed (ANSI X12, EDIFACT, etc). How does SAP handle this?',a:'EDI subsystem (middleware) handles format translation, not SAP. SAP creates standard IDocs → EDI middleware (e.g., Seeburger, Sterling) translates to customer-specific format. Partner profiles (WE20) route IDocs to correct port → correct middleware channel per customer.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'S/4HANA output management with BRF+ — what can you do that NACE condition technique cannot?',a:'BRF+ supports: complex if/then/else logic (not just condition record lookup), decision tables with multiple dimensions, function calls, expression evaluation, template-based output channel selection. Example: "if order value > 10L AND customer is VIP AND country is US → send email in English with premium template." Condition technique cant combine these dynamically.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Design output suppression logic: internal transfer orders should NOT generate customer-facing confirmations.',a:'Requirement routine (VOFM): check document type. If doc type = internal transfer → return false (suppress output). Assign this requirement to the customer confirmation output type. Internal orders silently skip the confirmation. Alternatively: dont maintain condition records for internal doc types.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Customer receives duplicate outputs — two copies of every order confirmation. Investigation?',a:'(1) Check if manual output was added on top of auto-determined output (two entries for same output type). (2) Check condition records — overlapping records at different access levels (e.g., Customer+DocType AND DocType only both triggering). (3) Check if output was reprocessed after initial send. (4) Check: is RSNAST00 running twice on the same selection? Fix: audit condition records for overlaps, check output log for duplicate entries.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Client wants to implement e-invoicing with digital signature as per government mandate. SAP approach?',a:'(1) Output type for e-invoice (XML format per government schema). (2) Adobe Form or custom program generates compliant XML. (3) Digital signature: SAP Document Compliance or ABAP crypto library adds signature. (4) Transmission: via government portal API (GSTN for India, SDI for Italy). (5) Response handling: store acknowledgment/IRN number on billing document. (6) S/4HANA: Document Compliance add-on handles country-specific requirements.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Performance issue: output processing for 10,000 daily invoices takes 4 hours. How to optimize?',a:'(1) Parallel processing: split RSNAST00 into multiple jobs by SalesOrg or document range. (2) Reduce form complexity (heavy graphics increase rendering time). (3) Check spool management — are old spools accumulating? (4) Email batch: aggregate emails rather than sending one per invoice. (5) Consider asynchronous processing with queuing. (6) For EDI: bulk IDoc creation instead of individual.',type:'troubleshooting'},
],
};

QB3.day9 = {junior:[
{q:'What are the three main copy control tables?',a:'VTLA: Order → Delivery. VTFL: Delivery → Invoice. VTFA: Order → Invoice (direct, for pro-forma, milestones, down payments).',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What does copy control define?',a:'Rules for how data flows between documents: which fields copy, how quantities transfer, which checks apply before copy, and which target document type is created.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'At which levels is copy control maintained?',a:'Header level AND Item level. Both must be maintained for the copy to work. Missing item-level entry = items dont copy even if header is configured.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Copying Requirement?',a:'An ABAP routine checked BEFORE the copy happens. E.g., requirement 001: goods issue must be posted before billing is allowed. If requirement fails, copy is blocked with an error message.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Data Transfer Routine?',a:'Controls HOW individual fields transfer from source to target document. Standard routines handle most cases. Custom routines for special field-level transformations.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What does Pricing Type B mean in copy control?',a:'Copy pricing from source document exactly as-is. Manual overrides are preserved. No re-reading of condition records. The target document gets exactly the same pricing as the source.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What does Pricing Type G mean in copy control?',a:'Copy pricing first, then redetermine from current condition records. If records changed since the source document, amounts will differ. Manual overrides from the source may be lost.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What does Pricing Type H mean in copy control?',a:'Full redetermination. Ignore source pricing entirely. Read all condition records fresh. Target document pricing is independent of source. Used when target should have current prices regardless of source.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Why does the invoice copy from delivery and not from order?',a:'Invoice must reflect what was ACTUALLY SHIPPED, not what was ordered. Between order and delivery: quantities may change (partial delivery), plants may change, batch assignments happen. Delivery is the physical truth.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What happens if copy control entry is missing?',a:'Cannot create the target document from that source. System throws error: "copy control not defined for this combination." The entry must exist for every valid source→target doc type combination.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is VOFM?',a:'Transaction for maintaining copy control routines (requirements, data transfer, formulas). Standard routines are SAP-provided. Custom routines can be created in the customer namespace.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the "complete reference" indicator in copy control?',a:'Forces full quantity copy. If set: must copy entire qty from source to target, no partial copy allowed. Used when business requires all-or-nothing processing.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the field rule in copy control?',a:'Controls individual field copy behavior: blank = dont copy, 1 = copy as-is, D = clear in target (dont bring over). Applied at field level for fine-grained control.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is copying requirement 001?',a:'Checks that goods issue (PGI) has been posted on the delivery before billing document can be created. Prevents invoicing before goods have left the warehouse.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is copying requirement 003?',a:'Checks that the sales order has been released (not blocked). Used in order→delivery copy to prevent delivery from blocked orders.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can you have different copy control rules for different doc type combinations?',a:'Yes. Each source doc type → target doc type combination has its own copy control entry. OR→LF has different rules than RE→LR (returns). Fully independent per combination.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the billing type assignment in copy control?',a:'VTFL specifies which billing document type is created from which delivery type. E.g., LF delivery → F2 invoice. LR returns delivery → RE credit memo. Controls billing type selection.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is quantity transfer in copy control?',a:'Determines how quantities flow: full qty, partial qty, remaining qty. Controls whether the target gets the full source qty, only the unprocessed portion, or a user-specified amount.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the update document concept in copy control?',a:'After copy, the source document status is updated. E.g., after creating delivery from order, order shows "partially delivered" or "fully delivered." Copy control triggers this status update.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Why is copy control critical for the entire O2C flow?',a:'Every step in O2C uses copy control: quote→order (VTAA), order→delivery (VTLA), delivery→invoice (VTFL), order→invoice (VTFA). Without proper copy control, the document flow chain breaks.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is VTAA?',a:'Copy control for sales document to sales document. E.g., Quotation→Order, Inquiry→Quotation, Contract→Order. Controls how pre-sales data flows into firm sales documents.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a reference document in billing?',a:'The delivery (for VTFL) or order (for VTFA) that the billing document references. The reference provides the data (quantities, pricing, customer) for the billing document. Without reference: no billing.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How does copy control handle returns?',a:'Returns have specific copy control entries: returns order (RE) → returns delivery (LR) via VTLA. Returns delivery → credit memo via VTFL. Quantity signs handled differently (returns = negative/credit).',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is collective billing and how does copy control enable it?',a:'Multiple deliveries combined into one invoice. Copy control must allow multiple source documents per target. Combined with billing date calendar on customer master and VF04 collective processing.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the billing date in copy control?',a:'Determines the date on the billing document. Options: from delivery date, from order date, from today, from customer billing calendar. Affects period assignment and revenue recognition timing.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the difference between order-related and delivery-related billing?',a:'Order-related (VTFA): billing directly from order (services, milestones). Delivery-related (VTFL): billing from delivery (physical goods). Different copy control paths, different triggering events.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can copy control routines be customized?',a:'Yes — via VOFM. Copy standard routine to customer namespace (number range 600-699). Modify the copy. Caution: custom routines create upgrade risk — SAP may change standard logic.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the VF04 billing due list?',a:'Lists all deliveries (or orders for VTFA) ready for billing. Uses copy control to determine readiness. Allows mass billing creation. Can be run in batch or interactively.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How does copy control prevent double billing?',a:'After billing, the delivery/order status updates to "billed." Copy control checks this status — if already billed, the document doesnt appear on VF04 again. Prevents duplicate invoices.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the intercompany copy control?',a:'IC delivery type → IC billing type has its own VTFL entries. IC-specific copying requirements (e.g., GI must exist), IC pricing type, and IC billing document type. Separate from external copy control.',type:'conceptual'},
],
senior:[
{q:'Client reports invoice amounts differ from order by 5-10%. No manual changes were made. Root cause?',a:'Copy control pricing type is G or H (redetermination). Between order creation and billing: condition records changed (new price list, expired discounts, updated surcharges). Fix: change pricing type to B (copy from source) if business wants order prices honored. Or: accept redetermination as the intended behavior.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Design copy control for a scenario where 30% of order value is billed at order creation (down payment) and 70% at delivery.',a:'Two copy control paths: (1) VTFA: order→billing with billing plan (30% milestone at order). Billing type: FAZ (down payment request). (2) VTFL: delivery→billing for remaining 70% after PGI. Billing type: F2. Down payment clearing happens on the 70% invoice automatically. Pricing type B on both to preserve order prices.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Custom VOFM requirement routine worked in ECC but fails in S/4HANA. Investigation?',a:'(1) S/4HANA may have enhanced standard routines — does the custom routine conflict? (2) Check: data model changes — are the tables/fields referenced still valid in S/4? (3) Check: new prerequisite checks added by SAP in the standard flow. (4) Compare routine logic line by line between ECC and S/4 versions. (5) S/4 may require additional checks (e.g., compliance, e-invoicing) that the custom routine bypasses.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Delivery for 100 units exists with PGI posted. Invoice created for 100. Customer then returns 20 units. Complete document flow?',a:'Returns Order (RE) for 20 → Return Delivery (LR) for 20 (goods receipt) → Credit Memo billing for 20 (via VTFL from returns delivery). Original order/invoice remain unchanged. Credit memo references the return delivery, not the original invoice directly. Document flow shows both chains from the same customer.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client wants to bill based on customer-requested frequency: some weekly, some monthly, some per-delivery. How does copy control support this?',a:'Per-delivery: standard VTFL, bill immediately after GI. Weekly/Monthly: billing date calendar on customer master controls when deliveries become due for billing. VF04 billing due list run weekly/monthly picks up deliveries whose billing date has arrived. Copy control allows combining multiple deliveries into one invoice (collective billing). No copy control change needed — its a customer master + VF04 scheduling design.',type:'scenario'},
],
};

QB3.day10 = {junior:[
{q:'What is the Transfer of Requirements (TOR)?',a:'Mechanism where sales order demand is transferred to Materials Management/Production Planning. The SO creates a requirement that MRP processes into planned orders, which convert to production orders or purchase requisitions.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What determines the requirement type?',a:'Item Category + MRP Type on the material master. This combination resolves to a Requirement Type, which determines the Requirement Class and planning behavior.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the difference between individual and collective requirements?',a:'Individual: stock earmarked for a specific SO (Make-to-Order). Other orders cannot consume it. Collective: stock available to anyone (Make-to-Stock). MRP plans for aggregate demand.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Make-to-Stock (MTS)?',a:'Produce to warehouse based on forecasts or collective demand. Stock is unrestricted — any customer order can consume it. Standard for high-volume, standard products.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Make-to-Order (MTO)?',a:'Produce specifically against a customer order. Stock is earmarked (individual requirement). Other orders cannot use it. Used for custom products or customer-specific production.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the MRP Type on the material master?',a:'Controls how MRP processes the material. PD = MRP with planned orders. VB = manual reorder point. ND = no MRP. The MRP type is one of two inputs (with item category) to requirement type determination.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What does Schedule Line Category F mean?',a:'Planning-relevant. Creates an MRP requirement when a sales order is created. This requirement triggers the supply chain to respond (production planning or procurement).',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What does Schedule Line Category C mean?',a:'Not planning-relevant. No MRP requirement created. Used when the material is available from stock and no replenishment trigger is needed from the SO.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What happens if TOR is not configured correctly?',a:'MRP ignores the SD demand. No planned orders, no production orders, no purchase requisitions. Material will not be available for delivery. Common: requirement type not set to planning-relevant.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How does TOR connect to ATP?',a:'TOR creates the DEMAND in MRP. ATP checks if SUPPLY can meet it. They work together: TOR says "I need 100 units by Jan 15," ATP says "only 80 available by then."',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Planned Order?',a:'MRP output: a system-suggested production or procurement action. Not yet a firm commitment. Must be converted to: Production Order (make), Purchase Requisition (buy), or Process Order (process manufacturing).',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the MRP Run?',a:'Program (MD01/MD02) that processes all requirements and supply data to generate planned orders. Must be EXECUTED after SO creation for TOR to take effect. Requirements dont auto-trigger production.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can a schedule line directly create a Purchase Requisition?',a:'Yes — schedule line category with "automatic PO" or direct procurement indicator. Bypasses MRP planning run. The PR is created at SO entry. Used for third-party and specific make-to-order scenarios.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the Requirement Class?',a:'Determines planning characteristics: individual vs collective requirements, requirement type assignment, availability check scope, account assignment category. Derived from requirement type.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Total Replenishment Lead Time?',a:'Sum of: in-house production time + planned delivery time (external procurement) + GR processing time. Used by ATP to determine when replenishment can fulfill demand.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the end-to-end O2C Pre-sales flow?',a:'Inquiry (IN) → Quotation (QT). Both are optional pre-sales steps before the firm Sales Order (OR). Quote has validity dates and can be copied to order via VTAA copy control.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the BOM explosion in relation to TOR?',a:'When a BOM material is ordered, the system can explode the BOM to identify components. TOR then creates requirements for each component. Timing (at order vs delivery) depends on item category config.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the Availability Date in the context of TOR?',a:'The date by which MRP must make the material available. Calculated by backward scheduling from the delivery date. TOR communicates this date to MRP as the requirement date.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How do mixed MTO and MTS items work on one order?',a:'Each line item independently determines its requirement type from item category + MRP type. MTO line = individual requirement. MTS line = collective requirement. Both on the same order, handled independently.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Scheduling Agreement in SD?',a:'Long-term contract with periodic delivery schedule (VA31). Each schedule line creates an MRP requirement via TOR. MRP plans production/procurement against the schedule. Used for regular recurring supply.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the third-party TOR flow?',a:'Item category TAS: schedule line creates a Purchase Requisition to an external vendor (not a production requirement). PR → PO → vendor delivers directly to customer. No inventory movement at seller.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the difference between production order and purchase requisition in TOR context?',a:'Production order = make it internally (PP module). Purchase requisition = buy it externally (MM module). MRP decides which based on material procurement type (internal manufacturing vs external procurement).',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is NETCH vs NEUPL in MRP?',a:'NETCH = Net Change planning (only processes materials with changes since last run — faster). NEUPL = Regenerative planning (processes all materials — comprehensive but slower). NETCH is standard for daily runs.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the Firming Date in MRP?',a:'Date before which planned orders are "firmed" — MRP will not change or delete them. Protects near-term production schedule from being disrupted by each MRP run.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How does TOR handle order quantity changes?',a:'If SO quantity changes: requirement is updated. Next MRP run adjusts planned orders accordingly. If already converted to production order: production order must be manually adjusted.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the role of the Plant in TOR?',a:'The delivering plant on the SO line item is where MRP looks for/creates requirements. If the plant has MRP active for that material, TOR creates the requirement there. Wrong plant = requirement in wrong location.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Variant Configuration in TOR context?',a:'Configurable materials where customer selects specific features (color, size, options). Each unique configuration creates a unique individual requirement. Production order inherits the specific configuration.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the relationship between TOR and Availability Check?',a:'TOR creates DEMAND in MRP. Availability Check reads SUPPLY (stock + receipts — requirements) to confirm. Both must be correctly configured. TOR without correct ATP scope = unconfirmed orders.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Pegging relationship?',a:'Links specific supply (production order, PO) to specific demand (SO requirement). Shows which supply element is fulfilling which demand. Useful for MTO tracking.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is consumption-based planning vs requirement-based planning?',a:'Consumption-based: replenish based on historical usage (reorder point, forecast). Requirement-based: replenish based on actual SO demand via TOR. Most SD-relevant scenarios use requirement-based.',type:'conceptual'},
],
senior:[
{q:'Client says production is not being triggered for new sales orders. MRP seems to ignore SD demand. Systematic diagnosis?',a:'(1) Check requirement type: is it planning-relevant? Trace: item category + MRP type → requirement type → requirement class → planning indicator. (2) Check MRP type on material: ND = no MRP. (3) Has MRP run (MD01/MD02) been executed AFTER the order was created? (4) Check: is the material extended to the delivering plant with MRP views? (5) Check: is the plant set up for MRP? Most common: MRP run not executed or requirement type not planning-relevant.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Design TOR for a company that has both standard catalog products (MTS) and custom-engineered products (MTO) on the same orders.',a:'Catalog products: item category TAN with MRP type PD → collective requirements (MTS). Custom products: item category with individual requirement indicator and MRP type PD → individual requirements (MTO). Both on same order. MRP plans differently: catalog = aggregate demand. Custom = specific SO-linked production order with potentially variant configuration.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client has 30-day production lead time but promises customers 15-day delivery. How does TOR work here?',a:'ATP checks against planned/existing supply. If current stock + expected receipts within 15 days cover the demand, order confirms. If not: order confirms for a date 30 days out (production lead time). To promise 15 days: either maintain safety stock, start production speculatively (MTS), or use aATP with alternative sourcing. TOR creates the requirement but cant change physical production speed.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Third-party scenario: SO creates PR, but procurement team says PR quantities dont match SO. Root cause?',a:'Check: (1) Schedule line category — does it have the correct PR generation settings? (2) Is the PR creation per line item or consolidated? (3) Unit of measure conversion issues between SD and MM. (4) Minimum order qty on vendor master creating rounded-up PR qty. (5) Check if multiple schedule lines exist on the SO item (each creates a separate PR line).',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Explain BOM explosion at order vs delivery — trade-offs and configuration.',a:'At order: customer sees components on confirmation, can modify component selection, component-level pricing possible, ATP checked per component. At delivery: more flexibility for last-minute changes, simpler order document, warehouse determines components. Trade-off: visibility vs flexibility. Config: item category "structure scope" and BOM explosion indicator determine timing.',type:'conceptual'},
],
};

export default QB3;
